params.comment:
The stack trace captured in the logs pinpoints the exact failing call: `Accounting.Consumer.ProcessMessage(Message message)` at `/src/Accounting/Consumer.cs:line 102`, which calls `DbContext.Add[TEntity](TEntity entity)` on a new `OrderEntity` graph. EF Core throws `InvalidOperationException` ("cannot be tracked because another instance with the same key value for {'Id'} is already being tracked") because `Add()` unconditionally tries to start tracking a *new* entity with a key that may already be tracked (by a prior attempt within the same DbContext scope, or by a concurrent handler).

Recommended code fix for `Consumer.cs:102` (exact line to confirm once source access is available — not reachable from this sandbox):
```csharp
// Instead of:
_dbContext.Add(orderEntity);

// Use an idempotent upsert, e.g.:
var existing = await _dbContext.Set<OrderEntity>()
    .FirstOrDefaultAsync(o => o.Id == orderEntity.Id);
if (existing is null)
{
    _dbContext.Add(orderEntity);
}
else
{
    _dbContext.Entry(existing).CurrentValues.SetValues(orderEntity);
}
await _dbContext.SaveChangesAsync();
```
This makes reprocessing the same `orderId` (whether from Kafka redelivery or a retry) a no-op/update rather than a tracking conflict, which would have prevented the entire 2.5-hour storm even before the bounded-retry/DLQ safety net is in place. Confirm the DbContext's lifetime is scoped per-message so concurrent handlers don't share tracked state.
